llo  life cx is a brand name that we've given  to it  which is absolutely small it's like 64  square foot eight feet by eight feet  which can be installed anywhere  a general primary consultation along  with medication  and investigation the whole thing we are  doing for as low as four dollars  for four dollars you get a doctor tests  and your medicines  which is less than 300 rupees in india  is the cheapest the most affordable  primary healthcare system that we are  aware of  the mini clinics like the one here  called hello life cx  cost around 25 000 us dollars to build  and can be set up in under a day  glocal has 240 of them around india  as well as 11 larger hospitals with  emergency services in rural areas  including the app the company is one of  the largest telemedicine operators in  the world  the biggest problems of the poor  of the marginalized of remote areas  okay will not be solved by poor  technology  but to be able to deliver healthcare in  three dollars or five dollars  your technology has to be absolutely  phenomenal  that can squeeze out the maximum amount  of data  the decision making the ability to  deliver everything has to be so  productive and so efficient that you are  able to create a sustainable margin  within that five dollars  so to solve the problem with the poor  it's a bad thing to do  when you think of putting in a cheap  thing  you have to be able to put the most  expensive  piece of technology which can create so  much of productivity  that the product itself can be cheaper  in the hands of the person  there are 7.8 billion people on this  planet  it was becoming inevitable that  technologies will have to provide a  solution that whatever can be done  without the doctor having to travel or  the patient having to travel  i think this pandemic has only catalyzed  this movement  [Music]  certainly in india and in a lot of asian  african countries  when people are poor and the money is  less healthcare  is sought only for the wage earner  because this guy is earning he's earning  member of the family if he falls ill  they will seek healthcare  but for the old for the women and the  children they would be neglected  and we are seeing and this is probably  the most gratifying thing that you see  that almost 50 percent of the patients  are women which it should be because you  are 50 50 in the population  so which means that it has increased the  equity in the society  the justice in the society so healthcare  access is no longer limited to wage  earner  or the upper caste or the privileged  members or the richer members of the  society but everybody  it is coming in faster it is bringing  the cost down so reducing poverty and  the biggest thing is that is available  everywhere  you

